## Scope, precedence and version

The base contract remains normative except for these precisely superseded singleton clauses in its section 4: “one owned pending review obligation per episode generation”; “create/retain owned pending review”; “one task retains appended changed-fact history”; and clearance closing “that generation's task.” Their replacement is: **one or more immutable owned follow-up tasks per episode generation, exactly one effective task for each distinct admitted full follow-up binding, append-only pending/closure events, and clearance closing every task of only its selected generation.** Other base references to a singular current task/summary are read as the complete task set. This is a real cardinality/lifecycle amendment, not merely a renamed implementation field. Each novel transition still advances patient/state/eligibility once, consent unchanged; additional tasks do not add increments. Cutover advances once per held patient, regardless of tasks. All other accepted authentication, gate, chronology, closure, atomicity, retry and no-restoration semantics remain normative.

This amendment is proposed before the first independent acceptance of LIVE-HOLD implementation or qualified production use. Retain initial contract `LIVE-HOLD-01/1.0.0`, capability `LIVE-HOLD-01/1.0.0`, service schemas 1.0.0 and product `LIVE-SAFETY-EXPORT/1.0.0`, with new exact composed specification/schema digests. No already accepted 1.0 production format is silently redefined: existing unaccepted checkpoint products/configurations remain historical, and current readers refuse their old binding rather than fallback. Observed v1/v2 schema/product/spec bytes and migrations 001–003 remain unchanged.

Generate a new `contracts/service/spec-v1.json` ordered manifest using the existing deterministic service `encoded()` JSON bytes. It contains contract/version, two ordered parts `{path,git_blob,lf_sha256}`, and precedence `second_part_overrides_only_named_task_and_trigger_binding_clauses`. Part 1 is the unchanged accepted executable R1; part 2 is this complete R1 amendment, with Git blob and LF-normalized hash taken from the frozen committed document. Neither document contains its own hash. The composed spec identity is SHA256 of encoded manifest bytes. `bindings-v1.json.live_hold` and `.live_safety` use this composed digest, and `.service_schema_set` binds regenerated new service schemas. The generated schema-set includes the new configuration types; an exporter check verifies both part identities, order, precedence and every resulting file. The superseded original supplement is historical evidence, not a third normative part.

Add required exact `service_contract` and `schema_binding` VersionRefs to installed LiveHoldAuthority; they must equal the copied compiled service bindings. Its persisted authority digest therefore binds these identities transitively through service_control, every operation/review and authority history. Startup and every current transaction compare compiled bindings, installed bundle and persisted generation/digests before access. Missing old fields or old bindings fail closed. Host configuration provisions the new immutable bundle and explicit migration version; copied service and learner modules/bindings carry the same composed spec/schema identities. Export manifests, trusted out-of-band authority and actual source/runtime provenance retain their existing exact fields with the new digests. No caller supplies a replacement authority or trust flag.

## Explicit trigger and follow-up configuration

Keep SourceGrant's account/service/archive patient, source ID/version, run/partition, exact archive ID/package digest/archive-authority digest, ownership ref, episode namespace and allowed invalidate operation. Replace its flattened trigger_rules/required_reviews/follow_up_owner_id/follow_up_policy/due_at/escalation_destination fields with nonempty `trigger_bindings`.

Each TriggerBinding is `{trigger_rule: VersionRef, required_reviews: nonempty list[Requirement], follow_ups: nonempty list[FollowUpBinding]}`. Requirement retains exact ID/definition, responsible doctor role, permitted evidence kinds and source IDs. FollowUpBinding is `{reference: ExactRef, owner_id, policy: VersionRef, due_at: aware datetime, escalation_destination}`. No default deadline, urgency, timer or computed clinical interval exists. Imported overdue dates remain overdue.

Reject duplicate exact grant keys including archive/package identity; duplicate trigger-rule refs within a grant; and conflicting content for a shared requirement ID or follow-up ref. Every shared requirement ID has identical complete definition/role/evidence constraints. Every configured follow-up owner equals the authenticated current owning doctor. Reusing one consistent binding across rules is permitted and deduplicated; ambiguous competing definitions are rejected at installation and admission.

For each bundle, resolve every exact positive requires_hold trigger. Each rule matches exactly one binding. Each trigger's declared requirement VersionRef appears among that binding's exact requirements. The union of matched requirements equals the bundle's required_reviews IDs/definitions/roles, without omission, conflict or substitution. Each matched binding's follow-up ref resolves in that exact registered package and appears in its trigger's and bundle's follow_up_refs. Conversely, every trigger/bundle follow-up has a matched exact binding; unconfigured extras reject. Full recursive current dependency heads, source/run/partition/patient/episode/ownership and clocks remain checked. Archive baseline/resolution tasks alone do not create live invalidations or tasks.

Active episode requirements, reasons and triggers retain their unresolved union. A new binding adds requirements/tasks, never removes or substitutes an earlier obligation. A conflicting definition rejects the entire transaction. Missing manual criterion for any active trigger or item blocks the whole mixed episode; a concern rule cannot satisfy a threshold item. Pending review includes every active item, with unsupported items pending. Whole-episode satisfied is unavailable until every exact obligation has an accepted rule and appropriate committed evidence.

## Additive persistence migration 004

Allocate `004_live_hold_followups.sql`; never change 001, 002 or 003. The existing `live_hold_tasks` table and its UNIQUE(episode_id,generation), IDs, payloads, foreign keys, immutable triggers and task events remain intact. It becomes the first/anchor task storage for a generation. Add these separate tables:

* `live_hold_additional_tasks`: immutable id primary key, patient/owner FKs, episode_id/generation composite FK to episodes, explicit due_at and exact immutable payload. It has no singleton episode/generation constraint.
* `live_hold_task_bindings`: episode_id/generation, full_binding_sha256 and canonical binding/provenance payload; task_id; nullable anchor_task_id FK to live_hold_tasks and nullable additional_task_id FK to live_hold_additional_tasks. Composite primary key `(episode_id,generation,full_binding_sha256)`, globally unique task_id, exactly one target FK nonnull, and task_id equals that selected target ID. Composite episode FK applies. This prevents duplicate effective follow-ups without weakening 003.
* `live_hold_additional_task_events`: immutable event ID, additional-task FK, operation ID and exact event payload. Existing anchor events remain in live_hold_task_events unchanged. Current task event views select the correct physical table; no polymorphic unvalidated FK is invented.

Use the same patient/episode/owner relation checks and immutable insert-only triggers for all new tables. Task IDs derive from episode ID/generation and full canonical binding including source archive/partition/ref, policy, owner, deadline and escalation; the first task's ID has an anchor prefix and later tasks a distinct additional prefix. Insert episode index, task rows, binding rows and pending events in the same transaction before any commit. The existing episode index is mutable and stores task_ids; immutable old tables are not rewritten. A distinct matched follow-up creates a distinct task; shared identical binding creates one. A later stronger transition appends new task IDs and pending changed-fact events to the retained set, never updates old task rows/deadlines. Review keeps all tasks active. Explicit clearance appends closure events to every selected-generation task and no other episode. Historical receipts retain exactly their original task set at that operation; current views/later receipts carry later additions.

Initial new-path activation runs 001→002→003→004 under the exclusive gate before routing. Ordinary startup refuses absent 004 and never migrates. Existing capable checkpoint DB upgrade requires explicit drain/stop of all workers, restart disablement, process/handle inventory, consistent backup and new private unrouted copy/instance/lock as in accepted section 2A. The offline migrator verifies exact old control/physical identity, preserved hashes, 001–003 bytes and old schema, applies 004 and validates/copies binding indexes within one transaction. Existing anchor task/event IDs and every original payload byte remain unchanged. It derives the old anchor's single exact FollowUpBinding from the stored original grant/bundle/task closure and compares policy/owner/due/escalation/hash. If there is no unique exact binding, upgrade refuses unrouted; it must not guess or silently split an ambiguous old aggregate. This bounded upgrade supports the actual prior checkpoint's one-exact-follow-up tasks; broader legacy aggregate conversion requires a separately reviewed explicit mapping.

During this migration, each old episode payload gains task_ids containing its preserved anchor ID; old current reviews are invalidated and held patients receive exactly one generation cutover increment/held decision/audit, consent unchanged. Saved old displays/receipts/reviews/events/raw archives are untouched. New authority generation and composed digests are installed atomically with 004 and binding indexes. New current startup verifies required migration set and new exact bundle/bindings. Old binaries with old bundles see generation/digest mismatch; passing new strict configuration to the old model also fails. A beforecommit fault rolls back schema/index/control/version changes and keeps new routing off; an aftercommit death recovers complete 004/new generation with stale-worker denial. Immutable-trigger/FK checks and protected-byte equality precede routing. No DROP/rename/rebuild of old FK targets is needed.

## Cutover additions require explicit exact amendment authority

A cutover may retain old unresolved tasks/obligations and strengthen exact matching requirements as accepted; mere provenance retention or shared namespace/run/partition/rule is never authority to add a task. Add a required explicit list `follow_up_amendments` to LiveHoldAuthority (empty is a deliberate valid list). No HTTP endpoint accepts it. Each entry is an administrative `FollowUpAmendment` with:

* exact service patient, service episode ID and active generation;
* expected prior authority generation/digests, prior trigger_set_sha256 and obligation_set_sha256;
* exact original source key `(episode_namespace,run_id,partition_id,archive_episode_id)` and full original source ID/version plus current owner identity;
* exact new source grant selector including archive ID/package hash/archive-authority hash and ownership ref; exact same-episode `source_bundle_ref`;
* the exact FollowUpBinding to add and an administrative authorization VersionRef. This is a structural task authorization in the immutable host-installed registry, not a scientific clearance criterion or a caller statement.

Under the old gate and current old-generation control, the offline administrator first reads the entire target and dependency set from committed storage, then samples actual cutover R; repeat every predicate after obtaining the SQLite writer transaction. The target must still be that active episode/generation with exact prior hashes and prior authority. The new grant must uniquely belong to the new installed registry, permit the target account/patient/source/run/partition/namespace, and match exact stored verified package/manifest/archive authority bytes. The source bundle must carry the target archived episode ID and same patient/run/partition/source; owner/ownership evidence must resolve exactly to the current doctor. Its follow_up_refs must explicitly include the amendment's exact follow-up record. That task must be a current nonretracted head in the configured admitted package series, source event/recorded/available and source cutoff <=R, and have exact configured owner/due/escalation. Its policy ref is the explicitly installed amendment policy, persisted with its provenance. All closure hashes/raw bytes and source scopes are reverified from committed original archive storage and configured files; uncommitted/future/stale refs reject.

This explicit amendment associates a new follow-up with the old active trigger set even when the original trigger did not name it. It does not claim that the old source said otherwise. The new same-episode source bundle provides explicit follow-up association; the administrative amendment provides current task-addition authority. Archived resolve_to_assessment may supply task provenance but never live review, clinical clearance, readiness or task closure. To avoid silently bypassing a new positive transition, every enter/change bundle in that amendment package must already have a committed matching invalidation semantic identity, or the cutover refuses and requires ordinary authorized coordinator projection first. No newly discovered positive trigger is silently consumed by the cutover task amendment.

The target task is added only if its full binding is novel; the authority_changed audit/operation records amendment ref, exact package/bundle/follow-up identities, committed-read proof at R, and resulting complete task/obligation set. Replay of an already installed generation is refused; recovery/retry of its activation ID returns/checks its committed identity without inserting a duplicate. Revoked old grants leave old tasks/requirements held and unsupported; they do not authorize additions. If no explicit amendment targets an episode, no task is added there merely because another new grant shares its namespace/rule. Foreign episode E2's bundle/ref cannot match E1's full source key. Cross-package positive is allowed only with this exact new grant, same archived episode/owner/source, explicit amendment and committed semantic/closure checks; otherwise reject, not skip an ambiguous requested addition.

Requirement strengthening at cutover uses the exact recorded episode source key, full source ID/version and active trigger-rule bindings of explicitly installed grants; it does not use string membership in a serialized key. Preserve all old definitions/tasks, reject substitution, invalidate prior reviews, append pending task events and one authority_changed fact. Removed definitions remain unsupported. New task count does not alter the one cutover increment per held patient.

